WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.803 --> 00:00:10.230 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to the North America capitals, episode three of five. First, I clear
the chosen names for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:10.881 --> 00:00:18.730 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Four capitals of the great Caribbean islands: Cuba, Jamaica and the two halves
of Hispaniola.

3
00:00:19.396 --> 00:00:22.078 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Havana into view.

4
00:00:25.202 --> 00:00:31.425 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Havana stands on the north coast of Cuba, on a bay that opens towards the
Florida Straits.

5
00:00:32.082 --> 00:00:50.730 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Founded on its present site in 1519, it kept a walled Spanish city behind its
ramparts for four centuries. Old Havana and its fortifications are a UNESCO
site, and its Malecón, a seawall promenade of about eight kilometres, runs
along the sea.

6
00:00:51.385 --> 00:00:58.374 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Havana as the walled city on the bay, with the Malecón running along
the sea.

7
00:00:59.076 --> 00:01:01.328 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Kingston into view.

8
00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:12.702 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Kingston stands on the south coast of Jamaica, across the harbour from the old
pirate town of Port Royal.

9
00:01:13.353 --> 00:01:25.126 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Founded in 1692, after an earthquake sank Port Royal, it has one of the
largest natural harbours in the world, and Bob Marley's home is now a museum
here.

10
00:01:25.778 --> 00:01:32.378 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Kingston as the harbour city that rose when the earthquake sank Port
Royal.

11
00:01:33.046 --> 00:01:35.519 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Port-au-Prince into view.

12
00:01:38.629 --> 00:01:43.958 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Port-au-Prince stands on the bay of the same name, on the southern coast of
Haiti.

13
00:01:44.619 --> 00:01:57.750 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Named for a French ship, Le Prince, that anchored in its bay, it was founded
by the French in 1749; an earthquake in 2010 destroyed much of it.

14
00:01:58.407 --> 00:02:07.788 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Port-au-Prince as the ship-named capital, founded in 1749 and largely
rebuilt after the earthquake of 2010.

15
00:02:08.541 --> 00:02:11.176 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Santo Domingo into view.

16
00:02:17.678 --> 00:02:24.841 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Santo Domingo stands on the south coast of Hispaniola, on the island the
Columbus family claimed first.

17
00:02:25.501 --> 00:02:38.423 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its cathedral, finished in 1540, is the oldest in the Americas, and its
Alcázar de Colón was the home of Diego Columbus, Christopher's son.

18
00:02:39.082 --> 00:02:46.454 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Santo Domingo as the oldest cathedral in the Americas, and the house
of Columbus's son.

19
00:02:50.985 --> 00:03:01.260 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Havana stands on the bay in the west, Kingston on the south coast of Jamaica,
and Port-au-Prince and Santo Domingo share Hispaniola between them.

20
00:03:01.914 --> 00:03:13.861 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the walled city and the Malecón, the harbour that rose when the
earthquake sank Port Royal, the ship-named capital rebuilt after 2010, and the
oldest cathedral in the Americas.
